          "id": "e0b9f833f2592dc149a84d7bd3a36e7282464417",
          "message": "fix: prevent SIGABRT during parallel BM25 index build merges (#5328)\n\nRefs #5218\n\nWhen merge_foreground fails during a parallel CREATE INDEX on a large\ntable (~20M rows), Tantivy's IndexWriter::Drop flushes the outer\nBufWriter (WritePtr), which calls\nSegmentComponentWriter::write()/flush() and re-enters the PostgreSQL\nbuffer manager while the thread is already unwinding — causing a\ndouble-panic and SIGABRT (signal 6).\n\nRoot cause: Tantivy's open_write wraps our SegmentComponentWriter in a\nconcrete std::io::BufWriter (WritePtr = BufWriter<InnerWritePtr>). On\ndrop, that outer BufWriter unconditionally flushes, calling back into\nour writer's write()/flush() which hit the PG buffer manager. If the\nbuffer manager raises an ERROR during cleanup, pgrx converts it to a\nsecond panic while the first is still unwinding, and Rust aborts with\nthread caused non-unwinding panic. aborting.\n\nFix: Guard SegmentComponentWriter::write() and flush() with\nstd::thread::panicking() so they no-op during unwind, preventing the\ndouble-panic. This matches the existing PanicSafeBufWriter pattern\nalready used in the same file. terminate_ref is not affected because\nit's gated by AntiCallToken, which Tantivy only produces on the explicit\ncommit path — never from Drop.\n\nTest: Added writer_noops_during_unwind which creates a\nSegmentComponentWriter, triggers a panic via catch_unwind, and verifies\nthat write() and flush() return Ok without re-entering PG during unwind.\n\nThe success path is completely unchanged. Only segment_component.rs is\nmodified. Orphaned file cleanup from failed merges is left for a\nfollow-up.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mithun Chicklore Yogendra <mithun.cy@gmail.com>",
