          "id": "eda623390fbd3ecadbef87f3b4a9355752c8eed9",
          "message": "fix: Join Scan returned the LIMIT as the count(*) OVER () total (#5575)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #5561\n\n## What\n\nStop the Join Scan from pushing the query's LIMIT into its scan when a\nplan\nnode above the join needs every joined row. Before this fix, `count(*)\nOVER ()`\nreported the LIMIT as the total, row-reducing SRFs returned too few\nrows, and\nGROUP BY without aggregates returned too few groups.\n\n## Why\n\nThe Join Scan applies the LIMIT as TopK inside the scan, taking it from\nthe\nraw parse tree even when the planner marked it unpushable\n(`limit_tuples = -1`). The safety checks from #4769 cover post-filters\nbut\nnot upper-planner nodes, so a WindowAgg, ProjectSet, or Group above the\nscan\nreceived only the pre-cut rows.\n\n## How\n\n`is_limit_pushdown_safe` now also declines when the query has window\nfunctions, target-list SRFs, GROUP BY, GROUPING SETS, or HAVING — the\nsame\nconditions for which the planner sets `limit_tuples = -1`. The parse\nflags\nare checked instead of `limit_tuples == -1` itself, which also means\n\"parameterized LIMIT\" and would needlessly disable the Join Scan for\nevery\n`LIMIT $1` query. Without a pushable LIMIT the Join Scan declines and\nthe\nstandard plan returns correct results.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/tests/joinscan_limit_safety.rs`: each decline case fails without\nthe\nfix, each guard passes with and without it (both verified by execution).\n\n| Test | Without fix | With fix |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| window count = true total | FAILED (total = LIMIT) | ok |\n| row-reducing SRF fills the LIMIT | FAILED (3 of 5 rows) | ok |\n| GROUP BY returns full groups | FAILED (collapsed) | ok |\n| plain pagination keeps Join Scan | ok | ok |\n| plain DISTINCT keeps Join Scan | ok | ok |\n| parameterized LIMIT keeps Join Scan | ok | ok |\n| subquery LIMIT below outer window keeps Join Scan | ok | ok |\n\nFull pg_regress passes with no expected-output changes.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nFour adjacent suspect cases (DISTINCT ON, `FETCH ... WITH TIES`, row\nlocking,\ndeferred DISTINCT) are reproduced and tracked in #5572.",
