          "distinct": true,
          "id": "5285af063793b339308fc6da5358e4cbeddbdb2a",
          "message": "feat: solve Param/SubPlan expressions on leader before MPP dispatch (#5511)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\nCloses #5445\nCloses #5918\n\n## What\n\nEnables MPP `JoinScan` execution when search predicates contain runtime\n`Param`s or `SubPlan`-backed PostgreSQL expressions. It also fixes\ncorrelated `JoinScan` rescans so each outer value can relaunch MPP\nwithout retaining worker, relation, buffer-pin, or `TupleDesc` resources\nfrom the previous execution.\n\nAggregateScan behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Why\n\nMPP workers do not have the PostgreSQL `PlanState` and `ExprContext`\nrequired to evaluate these expressions. They must be solved on the\nleader before DataFusion planning and worker dispatch.\n\nCorrelated rescans additionally need a complete execution boundary.\nResetting only parameter state left the previous MPP launch and relation\nstate alive, so later loops could execute serially while resources from\nthe first loop remained retained.\n\n## How\n\n- Recursively visits every `SearchQueryInput` in `JoinCSClause`,\nincluding both source-local and join-level predicates.\n- Preserves a pristine `JoinCSClause` and a pre-`setrefs` `custom_exprs`\nsnapshot for execution-time restoration.\n- On every execution, restores the pristine clause, solves the current\nPostgreSQL expressions on the leader, and rebakes the logical plan\nbefore physical lowering.\n- Sizes DSM from the freshly rebound serialized plan and dispatches the\nresulting distributed physical fragments. If a short launch declines, it\nrebakes provider metadata for a genuinely serial fallback.\n- On rescan, drains and joins workers, drops the stream, physical plan\nand runtime, clears relation state, restores the pristine clause, and\nre-arms MPP for the next value.\n- Treats rebake/codec failures as invariant violations instead of\nsilently continuing with unresolved plan bytes.\n\n## Trade-offs and scope\n\n- A correlated rescan performs full worker teardown, logical/physical\nreplanning, runtime recreation, and worker relaunch for each outer row.\nThere is currently no protocol for replacing a live worker plan in\nplace.\n- `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` retains worker metrics from only the final rescan\nloop.\n- Caching execution-time lowering is a performance follow-up.\n- Parameters inside supported search-query paths are handled. Parameters\nembedded in non-search cross-table PostgreSQL expressions remain\nunsupported because of the pre-existing `setrefs`/`custom_exprs`\ntranslation limitation.\n- Shared AggregateScan/JoinScan lifecycle unification is outside this\nPR.\n\n## Tests\n\n`mpp_joinscan.sql` covers:\n\n- forced-generic heap-filter rebinding with distinct values (`6` then\n`7`);\n- two parameterized sources with distinct bindings (`(6, 6)` then `(7,\n21)`);\n- join-level predicate rebinding (`1` then `101`);\n- InitPlan MPP/serial result equivalence;\n- correlated lateral rescans returning the value-specific rows while\nretaining `DistributedExec`;\n- worker metrics and absence of an outer PostgreSQL `Gather`.\n\nFocused `mpp_joinscan` regressions pass locally on PostgreSQL 17 and 18;\n`mpp_worker_sizing` and the AggregateScan MPP regression suite pass on\nPostgreSQL 18.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mithun Chicklore Yogendra <mithun.cy@gmail.com>",
