          "id": "d43ed03fc46fe8bec0d86ea8e003828e61f85e4d",
          "message": "chore: close the JSON blind spot, audit advisories in CI, and label the traps (#5868)\n\nSix cleanups from auditing the things dependency tooling doesn't look\nat. One of them turned out to be a live bug.\n\n## `docs/docs.json` is invalid JSON on main\n\n```\n$ python3 -c \"import json; json.load(open('docs/docs.json'))\"\njson.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Expecting ',' delimiter: line 329 column 23\n```\n\nA missing comma before `\"deploy/cloud-platforms/dokku\"`. The page exists\n— it is just dropped from the documentation navigation.\n\n**Three separate layers should have caught it and none could:**\n\n1. `.gitignore` ignored `*.json` wholesale, so JSON was invisible to git\nby default.\n2. `.prettierignore` excludes `**/*.json`, so the formatter never parses\nit.\n3. The `check-json` pre-commit hook does catch it — but only on machines\nthat run the hooks.\n\nSo the comma is fixed, and `lint-format` gains a **Check JSON Validity**\nstep that parses every tracked JSON file. That is the layer that was\nmissing; it runs on every PR and needs no toolchain.\n\n## The `.gitignore` rule that caused it\n\n`*.json` sat under an `# SSH` heading next to `*.pem`. It swallowed\nevery JSON file in the repo — `docs/docs.json` needed an explicit `!`\nnegation, and `context7.json` is tracked only because someone\nforce-added it. Credentials are now matched by name, with the\n`detect-private-key` and `detect-aws-credentials` hooks as the actual\nbackstop.\n\n`package.json` / `package-lock.json` are now ignored deliberately:\n`docs/README.md` installs mintlify globally, so those are artifacts of a\nlocal `npm install`, not something the repo carries.\n\n## `cargo audit` in CI\n\nNothing watched the RustSec database, so an advisory could land against\nan untouched dependency and nobody would know. Runs daily and on\nmanifest changes, with the same Slack alert as the other scheduled jobs.\n\n`.cargo/audit.toml` documents the one ignored advisory — rkyv 0.7 is an\n*optional, unenabled* dependency of `rust_decimal`, unreachable from\n`cargo tree -i` on any target, and `rust_decimal` is already at its\nlatest release. Verified `cargo audit` exits 0 with it and still prints\nthe nine unmaintained warnings.\n\n## Labelling things that look like work but aren't\n\n- **bincode must not be \"upgraded\".** 3.0.0 is a tombstone release whose\n`lib.rs` is a single `compile_error!`, published because development\nceased after a harassment incident. 2.0.1 is the last usable version and\n`cargo outdated` will keep offering 3.0.0 forever. The manifest now says\nso — and notes that the `legacy()` config encodes data that lands on\ndisk, so any replacement has to be byte-compatible.\n- **`SearchTokenizer::Raw` / `KeywordDeprecated` can't just be deleted**\ndespite the deprecation attributes. `from_json_value` still accepts\n`\"raw\"` for indexes built before the switch, and `postgres::options`\nconstructs `Raw` for the default text field config. Removing them is a\nuser-visible change needing a migration — not something to do in a\ncleanup PR, so the reason is recorded next to them instead.\n- **`check-yaml` never passed** on the multi-document Kubernetes\nmanifests under `docker/`, so `prek run --all-files` could not go green\n— which is how hooks stop being trusted. It gets\n`--allow-multiple-documents`.\n- **The two `scripts/` helpers were referenced nowhere**, including\nCONTRIBUTING, which otherwise documents every test path in detail.\nDocumented now.\n\n## Test\n\n`prek run --all-files` is green on every hook (ShellCheck aside — it\nruns in docker, which isn't up locally; CI covers it). Both `check-yaml`\nand `check-json` were failing on clean `main` before this, which I\nconfirmed by stashing. `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy` and `cargo audit` all\nclean.\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Philippe Noël <21990816+philippemnoel@users.noreply.github.com>",
