          "id": "893f02a3bb9cb2f80d01f2e690e3b2e303f94813",
          "message": "ci(benchmarks): measure vector latency across all held-out query vectors (#5729)\n\nStacked on #5724.\n\nLatency for the cohere kNN queries was measured by repeating **one**\nhardcoded vector (the 12,766-char `ALTER DATABASE cohere.qvec` literal\nin `after_create_index.sql`) ten times. A vector's cost depends on which\ncells it routes to, so that reported the cost of that one vector, not of\nthe workload — every latency number in the sweep runs so far is\neffectively a sample of size one.\n\nRuns the query once per held-out query vector instead: the same ~100\nvectors recall already scores, read from the fixtures the sweep loads\n(no extra S3 fetch). A first pass over every vector warms the cache for\nall of them, then the measured pass records one sample each, so\n`samples` becomes a latency distribution rather than repeats of a single\npoint.\n\nReports p50/p90/p95/p99 in the benchmark extras and p50/p95 in the PR\ncomment:\n\n| query | target | 1m (p50/p95) | 10m (p50/p95) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `knn_top10_1pct` | 90% | `0.5` → 0.9170 · 9.63 / 13.10 ms | `0.45` →\n0.9070 · 17.29 / 24.80 ms |\n\nAlso drops the baked literal: the CLI binds a vector itself before the\ntwo EXPLAINs that need one (`get_query_id` and the plan log), and the\nmeasured passes rebind per vector, so which one seeds the plan changes\nno timing. The held-out set becomes the only source of query vectors for\nboth recall and latency.\n\nDatasets without vector fixtures (stackoverflow) are unaffected — they\nkeep the existing warm-up-then-repeat path.\n\nWorth knowing before merge:\n- The tracked gh-pages metric is still `mean`, so series names are\nundisturbed, but its meaning changed (mean over 100 vectors, not 10\nrepeats of one). p50 may be the better tracked metric — left alone\nrather than silently redefining `value`.\n- ~10x more executions per operating point (200 vs ~20). Worth measuring\nthe CI cost on the first run.\n- p99 from 100 samples is roughly the 2nd-worst value, so treat it as\nindicative; p50/p90/p95 are solid.",
