          "message": "refactor: migrate the workspace to Rust edition 2024 (#5804)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #\n\n## What\n\nMoves `workspace.package.edition` from 2021 to 2024, and makes all seven\nworkspace members build and lint clean under it.\n\nTwo commits, meant to be reviewed separately:\n\n1. **`refactor: migrate the workspace to Rust edition 2024`** — the\nedition bump\nand every hand-written change needed to compile and pass clippy under\nit.\n2. **`style: reformat with rustfmt style edition 2024`** — pure `cargo\nfmt --all`\n   output, no hand edits. Worth reviewing with whitespace hidden.\n\n`rust-toolchain.toml` is unchanged: 1.97.1 already supports edition\n2024.\n`Cargo.lock` is unchanged.\n\n## Why\n\nEdition 2024 has been stable since 1.85 and the pinned toolchain is well\npast\nthat. Staying on 2021 means the workspace can't use let chains, `gen`\nblocks, or\nthe newer `impl Trait` capture rules, and the gap only gets more\nexpensive to\nclose as the codebase grows. This is also the last edition bump that has\nto be\ndone before those features show up piecemeal in new code.\n\n## How\n\n### Mechanical breakages\n\n- `extern \"C\" {}` blocks must now be `unsafe extern \"C\" {}` (6 sites).\n- `#[no_mangle]` must now be spelled `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` (4 sites).\n- **`##` is a reserved token sequence in 2024**, which breaks the `###`,\n`##` and\n`##>` operator declarations. They are now written with the hashes\nseparated —\n`#[opname(pg_catalog.# # #)]`. This looks alarming but is safe: pgrx\nstringifies\n  the `opname` token stream and strips whitespace out of it\n  (`op_name.to_string().replacen(' ', \"\", 256)` in\n`pgrx-sql-entity-graph/src/pg_extern/operator.rs`), so the operator\nnames\nPostgres actually sees are unchanged. Comments at both sites explain\nthis.\n- Match ergonomics: an explicit `ref` / `ref mut` is no longer allowed\nwhere the\n  binding mode is already by-reference (8 sites). In `stressgres`'\n`MultiTransaction` dropping the `ref` flipped a binding from `&Server`\nto\n  `&mut Server`, so the use site reborrows as shared.\n- Tail-expression temporary scopes changed, so three temporaries that\nwere being\n  borrowed past their new drop point are bound to locals\n  (`merge_policy.rs`, `fast_fields/mod.rs`, `more_like_this.rs`).\n\n### `impl Trait` capture rules\n\nReturn-position `impl Trait` now captures every in-scope lifetime. Where\nthe\nopaque type does not actually borrow, this is opted out of with precise\ncapturing (`+ use<>`), which restores the 2021 meaning exactly — it\neither\ncompiles, meaning no lifetime was captured, or it fails. `acquire_mutex`\nis the\nclearest case: it returns an `AcquiredSpinLock`, a\n`#[repr(transparent)]` wrapper\naround a raw `*mut slock_t` with no lifetime, and the spurious capture\nalone\naccounted for ~30 borrowck errors across `postgres/mod.rs`.\n\nIn the `tests` fixtures, `arb_wheres`, `arb_numeric_comparison`,\n`arb_numeric_expr` and `arb_joins_and_wheres` now take their `tables`\nparameter\nas a named generic instead of `impl AsRef<str>`, because\nargument-position\n`impl Trait` cannot be named in a `use<...>` list and precise capturing\nrequires\nevery type parameter to be listed.\n\n### `unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn`\n\nThis becomes warn-by-default in 2024, and CI runs clippy with `-D\nwarnings`.\n`pg_search` is a thin Rust layer over the Postgres C API where nearly\nevery\nfunction is an `unsafe fn` whose body is unsafe end to end. `cargo fix\n--edition`\nwraps each of those ~200 bodies in a blanket `unsafe {}`, which adds no\ngranularity and would have made this diff unreviewable, so instead the\nlint is\nallowed at the crate root with a comment marking it as follow-up work to\ntighten\nper-module. No other crate needed it.\n\n### Let chains\n\nLet chains are stable in 2024, so `clippy::collapsible_if` starts firing\non\nnested `if let` / `if` (184 sites). Collapsed via `cargo clippy --fix`.\n\n## Tests\n\nNo behavioral change is intended; this is an edition migration, so the\nexisting\nsuites are the test.\n\nVerified locally against **PostgreSQL 16**, matching what CI gates on:\n\n- `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings --no-deps` —\nclean\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` — clean\n- `RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --no-deps\n--document-private-items` — clean\n- `cargo metadata --format-version 1 --locked` — clean\n\n**Caveat worth a reviewer's attention:** the lint job runs against PG\n18, and\nthis sandbox only had PG 16 headers available, so the\n`pg17`/`pg18`-gated code\npaths were never type-checked locally. I audited them statically instead\n— the\n`extern` block, `#[no_mangle]` and `##` greps are cfg-independent and\ncome back\nclean across the whole tree, and the `pg15`/`pg17`/`pg18`-only regions\ncontain no\n`ref` bindings, no `-> impl Trait` returns and no collapsible `if`s.\nThat covers\nevery category of breakage this migration actually hit, but it is an\naudit rather\nthan a compile, so the PG 18 lint run is the real check.\n\nI also reviewed the 22 `if let` sites that `cargo fix` flagged for the\nedition's\n`if let` scrutinee drop-order change, since that one is a silent\nsemantic change\nrather than an error. None are affected: the only lock-shaped case\n(`AdvisoryLock::conditional_lock_session` in `postgres/merge.rs`) only\nreaches its\n`else` branch when the value is `None`, and the rest are plain\n`Result`/`Option`\nof data.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude\nCode](https://claude.ai/code/session_01V2MXqNL7KqpKYTgY9HfDmD)_\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
