          "distinct": true,
          "id": "cf680b1b92d3fe5a34ea8da8013bcc90230c9526",
          "message": "chore: upgrade rand to 0.10 (#5860)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #\n\n## What\n\nBumps `rand` from `0.9.4` to `0.10.2` in `pg_search` and `tests`, and\nswaps five `Rng` imports plus one trait bound to `RngExt`.\n\nContinues the one-at-a-time major-version upgrades (after #5843 sysinfo,\n#5844 syn, #5849 rstest, #5853 proptest-derive, #5854 strum, #5856\ncmd_lib/emojis).\n\n## Why\n\n`rand` is one of three crates still behind a major, and the only\nremaining one in production code paths besides `bincode`. 0.10 released\nafter this upgrade sweep started.\n\n## How\n\n### Most of 0.10's churn doesn't touch us\n\n`choose_multiple`/`choose_multiple_fill`/`choose_multiple_weighted`\nrenamed to `sample*`, `SliceChooseIter` → `IndexedSamples`,\n`SeedableRng::from_os_rng` and `try_from_os_rng` removed,\n`TryRngCore::read_adapter` → `RngReader`, `Fill` reimplemented for\nelement types, `rand_chacha` replaced by `chacha20` behind `StdRng`.\n**Zero uses of any of it** across all crates. MSRV moves to 1.85 (we pin\n1.97.1) and edition 2024 is already the workspace default.\n\n### The change that does affect us isn't in the release notes\n\n**0.10 splits the `Rng` trait**, moving the ergonomic methods —\n`random`, `random_range`, `random_bool`, `sample_iter` — out to a new\n`RngExt: Rng`. Every call site here imports `Rng` solely to bring those\ninto scope, so a bare version bump fails to compile everywhere:\n\n```\nerror[E0599]: no method named `random` found for struct `StdRng` in the current scope\nhelp: trait `RngExt` which provides `random` is implemented but not in scope\n```\n\nFixed by swapping the five imports, plus widening one bound that a pure\nimport swap would have missed:\n\n```rust\n-fn pick_bulk_inserts(rng: &mut impl Rng) -> NonZeroUsize {   // calls rng.random_bool(0.5)\n+fn pick_bulk_inserts(rng: &mut impl RngExt) -> NonZeroUsize {\n```\n\n`RngExt: Rng`, so importing `RngExt` alone is sufficient — no site needs\nboth.\n\n### Seeded reproducibility\n\nThis is the property actually worth protecting. `querygen` does\n`StdRng::seed_from_u64(qgen_seed)`, and its own docs promise a failing\nrun can be replayed with `PARADEDB_QGEN_SEED=<n>`. Since **0.10 changed\nwhat backs `StdRng`** (rand_chacha → chacha20), a shifted stream would\nsilently invalidate every recorded seed while everything still compiled\nand passed.\n\nUpstream states the output is unchanged. That was verified rather than\nassumed — across seeds `0`, `1`, `42`, `12345`, `u64::MAX`, plus\n`from_seed([7; 32])`, comparing:\n\n- `random_range` on an inclusive f64 range (the `pg_seed` pattern) to 17\ndecimal places\n- `random_range` on exclusive i32 and usize ranges\n- `random_bool` sequences\n- `random::<u64>()`\n- `Alphanumeric` via `sample_iter`\n\n**Byte-identical between 0.9.4 and 0.10.2.** Existing\n`PARADEDB_QGEN_SEED` values still replay.\n\n## Dependency graph\n\nNo new version added: `rand 0.10.2` was **already in the lockfile**, so\nthis moves our two edges onto a copy already being compiled. `rand\n0.9.5` stays regardless — datafusion's subtree pins it. The lock diff is\nexactly those two edges.\n\n## Tests\n\nBoth crates reach lindera, whose build script downloads dictionaries\nfrom `lindera.dev` — blocked by egress policy in my environment — so\nneither compiles locally. The matrix jobs here are what build them.\n\n- equivalence harness above, which also compiles all three real\ncall-site shapes (`&mut impl RngExt` bound, `sample_iter(Alphanumeric)`,\n`random_range` on both range kinds)\n- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` — clean\n\n## Remaining after this\n\n| crate | current → latest | note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `lindera` | 1.5.1 → 4.0.1 | collapses the duplicate strum from #5854\n(4.0.1 needs strum 0.28); but drops the `compress` feature, so embedded\ndictionaries become raw `include_bytes!` — a `.so` size vs. startup\ntrade-off, and segmentation output needs checking |\n| `bincode` | 2.0.1 → 3.0.0 | on-disk encoding |\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude\nCode](https://claude.ai/code/session_01XEm6G1qKw694BQQqb9HBiU)_",
