          "id": "149a61ba8d15aae91cb3ec2967782d9746106f76",
          "message": "fix: resolve INSERT in read-only transaction error for pdb.alias typmod (#5320)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #5254 \n\n## What\nFix `cannot execute INSERT in a read-only transaction` error when using\n`pdb.alias('name')` in a `BEGIN TRANSACTION READ ONLY` block.\n\n## Why\n`pdb.alias` was using `generic_typmod_in` as its type modifier handler.\nWhen a bare alias name like `'bar_lower'` is passed, it gets stored in\n`_typmod_cache` under the key `[\"bar_lower\"]`. However, `CREATE INDEX`\nstores the same alias via `literal_normalized('alias=bar_lower')`, which\nuses the key `[\"alias=bar_lower\"]`. These two different keys meant the\ncache entry from index creation was never reused at query time so\n`pdb.alias('bar_lower')` always attempted a new `INSERT` into\n`_typmod_cache`, which PostgreSQL blocks inside a read-only transaction.\n\n## How\nGive `pdb.alias` its own typmod handler `alias_typmod_in` that\nnormalizes bare input `'bar_lower'` to `'alias=bar_lower'` before the\ncache lookup. This makes both `CREATE INDEX` and `SELECT` paths share\nthe same cache row, so no `INSERT` is ever needed at query time.\n\n- Set `custom_typmod = true` on the `pdb.alias` type definition to\ndisable the auto-generated `generic_typmod_in` hook\n- Add `alias_typmod_in` which normalizes the input and delegates to\n`save_typmod`\n- Register `alias_typmod_in` via `extension_sql!` as the `TYPMOD_IN`\nfunction for `pdb.alias`\n\n## Tests\nAdded `pg_search/tests/pg_regress/sql/issue_5254.sql` that creates a\ntable, builds a BM25 index with `literal_normalized('alias=bar_lower')`,\nthen runs a `SELECT` using `pdb.alias('bar_lower')` inside a `BEGIN\nTRANSACTION READ ONLY` block and verifies it returns rows without error.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Philippe Noël <philippemnoel@gmail.com>",
