          "id": "0effcd9eacee067be66d9a85894de997c9b69e35",
          "message": "feat: Push down SELECT DISTINCT into BM25 index scan (#5148)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #5102\n\n## What\n\nThis PR pushes `SELECT DISTINCT` into the ParadeDB index through\n`AggregateScan`.\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT a, b` is `GROUP BY a, b` with no aggregates, and\n`parse->distinctClause` is\nthe same `List<SortGroupClause>` as `parse->groupClause`. The\nupper-paths hook now also fires\nat `UPPERREL_DISTINCT`, resolves the two stages into one shape, and\nhands the existing GROUP BY\nmachinery a grouping with no aggregates. Postgres no longer streams\nevery matching row through\na `HashAggregate`.\n\n## Why\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT col FROM t WHERE col2 @@@ '…'` emitted every matching\nrow and let Postgres\ndeduplicate. On a table with millions of rows and hundreds of thousands\nof distinct values\nthat runs 2-10x slower than a plain btree `SELECT DISTINCT`.\n\n## How\n\n- **One grouping shape.** `GroupingShape::from_args` decides\nGROUP-BY-vs-DISTINCT once at the\nhook boundary and carries the target list, the row estimate and the\nflag. Nothing downstream\n  reads `args.stage`.\n- **Routing.** DISTINCT is GROUP BY forced to DataFusion. Tantivy's\n`TermsAggregation` is\nfaster for a low-cardinality GROUP BY, but its bucket cap would truncate\na high-cardinality\n  DISTINCT without saying so.\n- **Row estimate.** `create_distinct_paths` leaves `distinct_rel->rows`\nat zero, so the path\ntakes its estimate from the `Unique` paths Postgres already costed onto\nthe same relation.\n  Advertising one row would misprice everything planned above it.\n- **Join ownership.** JoinScan deduplicates a DISTINCT itself and\nmaterializes the heap late,\nso AggregateScan steps aside when JoinScan put a path on the join\nrelation. Asking whether\nthe path exists, rather than replaying JoinScan's gates, keeps a join\nthat JoinScan turned\ndown for its own reasons (a volatile predicate, say) from losing both\npushdowns.\n\n## What DISTINCT declines\n\nThe aggregation backend groups a fast field, which holds the column\nvalue. Postgres\ndeduplicates the expression value with the expression's collation. Those\nagree only for a\nplain column with a deterministic collation, so the rest falls back to\nPostgres:\n\n- Expressions: `upper(col)`, `a || b`, `col::text`, `meta->'tags'`.\nFollow-up in #5276.\n- Nondeterministic collations, where two different byte strings can\ncompare equal.\n- `DISTINCT ON`.\n- Columns with no fast field.\n\nEach decline raises a planner warning that\n`paradedb.check_aggregate_scan = off` silences.\n\n## Visible behavior change\n\n`SELECT DISTINCT` now reaches the aggregate path on base relations, so a\nquery that used to\nplan without comment can pick up a decline warning.\n`paradedb.check_aggregate_scan` defaults\nto on. This is how GROUP BY has always behaved.\n\nGROUP BY carries the same expression and collation exposure. That\npredates this PR and is out\nof scope here; #5703 is the fix for the collation half.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `select_distinct.sql`: 20 cases, each comparing the pushdown against\n  `paradedb.enable_aggregate_custom_scan = off`.\n- `mpp_aggregate.sql`: DISTINCT over a join, serial and MPP.\n- Warning-text updates in `aggregate_scan_expressions`, `issue_2745`,\n`issue_4667`,\n  `join_distinct`, `join_distinct_expr`, `topk-agg-facet`.\n\n## Note on the history\n\nRebased onto `main` and squashed. The nine original commits crossed a\ndesign change (the\ntwo-phase Tantivy path was added and later removed), so replaying them\none by one produced\nconflicts against code that no longer exists. Authorship is kept on the\nfeature commit.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Mohammad Dashti <mdashti@gmail.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-18T16:22:51-07:00",
