          "id": "c7bf2b656e18e1e6327f96a023417c4b2a40db0c",
          "message": "feat: added aggregate pushdown for `NUMERIC` columns. (#5932)\n\n## Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #4052\n\n## What\n\nThis PR pushes down `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, and `COUNT` on\n`NUMERIC(p,s)` columns of any precision, for both storage formats:\n`Numeric64` (scaled i64, `p <= 18`) and decimal-bytes (`p > 18`). The\ndriving case is `SUM` over `NUMERIC(78,0)` amount columns.\n\nIt also fixes two bugs on the DataFusion aggregate path (aggregate over\na join, both GUCs default on):\n\n- `SUM`/`AVG`/`MIN`/`MAX` on a `Numeric64` column returned the raw\nscaled integer (`12100` instead of `121.00`).\n- `SUM` on a decimal-bytes column failed the whole query with a planning\nerror instead of falling back.\n\n## Why\n\nNUMERIC aggregates were disabled on the Tantivy backend in #3997:\nTantivy aggregations compute in f64, which loses precision and\nmishandles the NaN/Infinity sentinels. The join path had no gate at all\nand aggregated the raw storage. #4052 proposed teaching Tantivy decimal\naggregation; the DataFusion backend gives the same result without a\nTantivy fork change.\n\n## How\n\n- Queries with numeric aggregates (or numeric `GROUP BY` keys) route to\nthe DataFusion backend of `AggregateScan`. `pdb.agg()` stays on the\nTantivy backend, which keeps rejecting NUMERIC fields.\n- Four UDAFs accumulate exactly: `numeric64_sum`/`numeric64_avg` sum the\nscaled i64 column in i128 (the scale travels as a plan literal),\n`numeric_bytes_sum`/`numeric_bytes_avg` decode decimal-bytes and sum in\n`BigDecimal`. All emit a decimal-bytes `Binary`, so NaN/Infinity and\narbitrary precision survive partial merges, parallel execution, and MPP\ndispatch. The UDAFs are registered in both plan codecs.\n- `AVG` evaluates to a `[count, sum]` blob. The final division runs\nthrough `AnyNumeric` on the Postgres side, so the result scale follows\nPostgres' division rules.\n- `MIN`/`MAX`/`COUNT` stay on the native aggregates: both storages are\norder-preserving with NaN sorting highest, matching Postgres. The same\nproperty lets `ORDER BY SUM(...) LIMIT k` use DataFusion's TopK directly\non the encoded sums.\n- Projection passes the declared scale so display matches Postgres\n(`3.30`, not `3.3`). This also fixes numeric `GROUP BY` key display on\nthe join path.\n- These shapes decline and fall back to Postgres: unbounded `NUMERIC`\n(index storage drops per-value display scale; `COUNT` still pushes\ndown), `HAVING` on a numeric aggregate, `SUM`/`AVG` with `DISTINCT`,\n`STDDEV`/`VARIANCE`, and TopK ordering by a numeric `AVG`.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `numeric_aggregate.sql`: all three storages, `GROUP BY` on text and\nnumeric keys, `ORDER BY` aggregate with `LIMIT`, NaN, empty result sets,\n`FILTER`, aggregate over a join, MPP legs, and each declined shape. Each\nresult is cross-checked against the Postgres fallback in the same file.\n- Unit tests for the shared accumulator: 78-digit exactness,\nNaN/Infinity combinations, i64 sentinel handling.\n- `numeric_pushdown.out` updated: `NUMERIC(18,18)`, `NUMERIC(5,-3)`, and\nprecision-only columns now push down; query results are unchanged.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: paradedb-github-app[bot] <282009505+paradedb-github-app[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Ming Ying <ming.ying.nyc@gmail.com>",
