          "distinct": true,
          "id": "63a7a931f65dabcb70700f3dadf0bf1b0742b8a4",
          "message": "feat(mpp): plan-first worker launch sized from df-d task fragments (#5756)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #5667\n\n## What\n\nReorders the MPP launch to PostgreSQL's canonical parallel-query\nsequence — build the plan, size by walking the plan, create the DSM,\nlate-bind pointers, launch — so the producer pool is sized from the\ndistributed plan's task fragments instead of a fixed GUC.\n`paradedb.mpp_worker_count` is removed.\n\nMPP worker count is now gated in three layers, each only able to shrink\nwhat the previous allows:\n\n1. **The plan (the data):** workers ≤ the widest producer stage's task\ncount — task counts derive from segment counts (#5657). A widest stage\nunder 2 tasks has no data parallelism: no launch at all, zero forks,\nserial run.\n2. **The config (PG's own GUCs):** workers ≤\n`min(max_parallel_workers_per_gather, max_parallel_workers)` — also the\non/off switch: below 2, MPP is inactive (`per_gather = 0` disables, PG's\nown convention).\n3. **The runtime (the pool):** if concurrent queries have drained\n`max_parallel_workers` and `LaunchParallelWorkers` grants fewer than\nrequested, the short-launch fallback (#5061) runs the query serially.\n\nAs one formula: `launched = clamp(widest-stage tasks, 2, min(per_gather,\nmax_parallel_workers))`, with serial fallback if PostgreSQL can't\ndeliver. The floor of 2 is the mesh-width invariant (every distributed\nplan is built for ≥ 2 streams), not a gate.\n\nMeasured on a 2-segment join with the cap at 8: **2 producers launched\ninstead of 7** (5 formerly idle), mesh DSM **384 MiB instead of ~3.5\nGiB**. A plan with nothing to distribute now forks **zero** workers\n(previously: full set forked, then aborted). Every launched worker\nprovably owns at least one fragment — *\"no fragments assigned;\nskipping\"* is unreachable by construction, not merely rare.\n\n## Why\n\nDirect answers to the issue's three investigation points:\n\n1. **Can worker allocation be deferred until after the physical plan and\nfragments exist?** Yes — the launch is leader-driven\n(`CreateParallelContext` via `ParallelProcessBuilder`, not Gather), and\nplanning happens in the same `exec_custom_scan` call, so the ordering\nwas ours to choose. The old spawn-before-plan order existed only to\noverlap worker fork with planning time.\n2. **How to determine the workers actually needed?**\n`max_producer_task_count(plan)` — the widest producer stage's task\ncount. Narrower stages multiplex onto the same procs (`proc_for_task`\nround-robin), and segments beyond the worker count are picked up through\nthe shared claim pool, so the widest stage is the exact answer.\n3. **Deprecate `mpp_worker_count` or repurpose as a maximum?**\nDeprecated entirely. The planner's ceiling and the operator throttle\nboth ride PG's own parallelism budget. This completes the arc started in\n#4827 (which introduced this GUC alongside `enable_mpp`, itself since\nremoved) and #5132 (which centralized the `>= 3` gate this PR\nre-expresses against the PG GUCs).\n\n## How\n\n- The leader's plan builds **address-free**; after the launch commits,\n`stamp_parallel_state` walks the built plan and binds the shared\n`ParallelScanState` pointer into the scan leaves (descending explicitly\nthrough `DistributedLeafExec` and `FilterPassthroughExec`, whose\n`children()` hide their inner nodes) — mirroring core PG's\nPlan-vs-PlanState split. Stamping sits after the last serial fallback,\nso a stamped plan cannot outlive its DSM.\n- The prepare/commit split, go-flag parking-across-planning,\n`MppLifecycle::Prepared`, and two now-dead pointer-courier fields\n(`JoinScanState.parallel_state`, `MppLeaderState.parallel_state`) are\ndeleted; the go flag remains solely as the short-launch abort (#5061).\nAll serial fallbacks, dispatch, mesh transport, and teardown paths are\nreused unchanged.\n- `MppLaunchTiming::explain_text()` is shared by JoinScan and\nAggregateScan, so both render the `MPP Launch: workers=…` line — making\nlaunched width observable and testable on both paths.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew `mpp_worker_sizing` regress asserts the sizing spec end-to-end:\n\n```\n1-segment join: ran serially (no MPP launch recorded)      ← + mpp_debug lock: no launch even attempted\n2-segment aggregate: launched exactly 2 producers\n2-segment join: launched exactly 2 producers               ← cap 8\n4-segment join at cap 2: launched 2 producers, results match serial\n```\n\nPlus: traversal unit/pg-tests for the stamp visitor; all six\npre-existing MPP regress suites pass byte-stable (data-path suites\npinned at a producer cap of 3 to keep expected plans unchanged);\n`mpp_cancel` teardown test passes; 270 lib tests pass.\n\n## Notes for reviewers (cc @stuhood @mdashti)\n\n- **Behavior changes to ratify:** (1) MPP is now reachable at stock PG\nsettings (`per_gather = 2`) wherever the join/aggregate custom scans are\nenabled; (2) there is no MPP-only kill switch short of `per_gather = 0`;\n(3) plans whose widest stage has a single task now run serially rather\nthan distributed-with-an-idle-worker.\n- **Known trade-off:** the fork-overlaps-planning latency optimization\nis forfeited; `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`'s `MPP Launch` line (`plan_us` vs\n`attach_us`) measures the cost.",
          "timestamp": "2026-08-02T11:04:46+05:30",
